#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162739} :activity/id #uuid "dc843eb0-59e2-5d15-bd81-c09a747c8de0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051069 :topic/id #uuid "7e5753ab-fcea-5d2f-ac49-4b475153e05c" :topic/math-academy-id 3027} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163212 :tutorial/content "A function $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is said to be an **into** function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X$.\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n> $f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X$, we have $f(x) \\neq y$.\n\nThe following diagram shows an example of an into function.\n\n![](images/ff/ff2c2b4099fc1cf30beb2d0f795049e55ad955b06a298d0bd93d8724e22cd0e3.png)\n\nAccording to the diagram, the element $4 \\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $4$.So, we conclude that there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=4$) contained within $Y$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x) \\neq y\n$$\n\nfor any $x$ in $X$.\n\nOn the other hand, the following is *not* an into function because every element in $Y$ has at least one element of $X$ that maps to it.\n\n![](images/12/120185557c1f1e2846dece02a4675c2952d1e6c3a01cd66a17458596302266ac.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "60b69e68-4b54-5b90-ad74-cf6aa0dadc52" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4820 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e005dc62-0efa-54ee-906c-34a5e9d6c724" :step/math-academy-id 181734 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162740}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162740 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161920 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161754 :question/id #uuid "2ac70ed6-4f03-59ae-9a3b-b8a93386507d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8021"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049745}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162565}] :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"} :step/id #uuid "1b33a7a7-bad5-529c-8e89-89c564737c01" :step/math-academy-id 181735 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162741}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162741 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161921 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161755 :question/id #uuid "346ff48a-b54f-56cc-b5b2-4437c7d3d94d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8022"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ad9700-5aaa-5b00-8d2f-acce6ed59c9a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050829}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162568}] :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "38c04fef-4547-57e5-ac3a-6aee5d534539" :step/math-academy-id 181738 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162742}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162742 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163213 :tutorial/content "Recall that $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X$:\n\n> $f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X$, we have $f(x) \\neq y$.\n\nConsider the function $f: \\Bbb R \\rightarrow [0,\\infty)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\mid x-1 \\mid +1\n$$\n\nNotice that the codomain is not $\\mathbb R$ but $[0,\\infty) \\subseteq \\mathbb R$. Let's show that $f$ is an into function.\n\nTo show that $f$ is into $[0,\\infty)$, let's take ${\\color{blue}0} \\in [0, \\infty)$. Notice that there is no number $x$ in the domain $\\mathbb{R}$ such that\n\n$$\ng(x)= \\mid x-1 \\mid +1={\\color{blue}0}\n$$\n\nIn fact,\n\n$$\n\\mid x-1 \\mid +1 \\geq 1\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThus, we have showed that $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ (e.g.,\n\n$$\ny={\\color{blue}0}\n$$\n\n) such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x) \\neq y\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ is an into function.\n\nThe graph of our function $f$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/11/110203c5a05e9e1b56bbd8483bd78e1be9245a7f1b73738e22ce469ce44f3af1.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "91165645-9f3f-5d72-a411-e1b6b0b0a3b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4821 :tutorial/title "Cases Where the Codomain Is a Real Interval"} :step/id #uuid "c77caab9-b4ab-5f2f-a6d4-b0ba66aacd5f" :step/math-academy-id 181741 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162743}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161922 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161756 :question/id #uuid "d3e8ba8d-5a83-5683-9e19-33f021459ceb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8023"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd378899-03ff-57a0-848c-11b93c8e5055" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050753}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162569}] :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval"} :step/id #uuid "2e11e568-ff34-53f6-aaf4-e4c7a0a71c7d" :step/math-academy-id 181742}] :activity/title "Into Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162180}]}
